data.content:
The lightest option depends on your target framework:

### .NET 8+ → use the built-in `TimeProvider`

No new types needed. Accept `TimeProvider` with a default of `TimeProvider.System`:

```csharp
public class InvoiceCalculator
{
    private readonly TimeProvider _clock;
    public InvoiceCalculator(TimeProvider? clock = null)
        => _clock = clock ?? TimeProvider.System;

    public bool IsOverdue(DateTimeOffset dueDate)
        => _clock.GetUtcNow() > dueDate;
}
```

In tests, use `Microsoft.Extensions.Time.Testing.FakeTimeProvider`:

```csharp
var fake = new FakeTimeProvider(new DateTimeOffset(2026, 1, 1, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero));
var calc = new InvoiceCalculator(fake);
Assert.True(calc.IsOverdue(new DateTimeOffset(2025, 12, 31, ...)));
fake.Advance(TimeSpan.FromDays(1)); // move time forward
```

### Pre-.NET 8 → a tiny `IClock` interface

```csharp
public interface IClock { DateTimeOffset UtcNow { get; } }
public sealed class SystemClock : IClock {
    public DateTimeOffset UtcNow => DateTimeOffset.UtcNow;
}
```

Then accept `IClock` the same way and supply a stub in tests.

---

**Why this over `Ambient Context` / static `Func`?**  Constructor parameters are explicit, discoverable, and don't create hidden shared state that causes the very order-dependence you're trying to fix.

Want me to apply the `TimeProvider` (or `IClock`) refactor to your `InvoiceCalculator`?
